{
  "status": "ok",
  "data": {
    "query": "Huawei Meng Wanzhou DOJ charges January 2019 China reaction extradition",
    "search_window": {
      "from": "2018-11-01",
      "to": "2019-01-30"
    },
    "sources": {
      "grounding": {
        "generic": [
          {
            "url": "https://nationalpost.com/news/politics/u-s-lays-criminal-charges-against-meng-wanzhou-and-huawei-confirms-it-will-seek-her-extradition",
            "title": "U.S. lays charges against Meng Wanzhou, confirms it will seek her ...",
            "snippets": [
              "# U.S. lays charges against Meng Wanzhou, confirms it will seek her extradition from Canada\nActing US Attorney General Matthew Whitaker announces a 13-count indictment of financial fraud charges against Chinese telecommunications manufacturer Huawei, as well as two affiliated companies and Huawei's Chief Financial Officer Wanzhou Meng, during a press conference at the Department of Justice in Washington, DC, January 28, 2019.",
              "## Sign In or Create an Account\n##### U.S. lays charges against Meng Wanzhou, confirms it will seek her extradition from Canada Back to video\nArticle content\n\nActing US Attorney General Matthew Whitaker announces a 13-count indictment of financial fraud charges against Chinese telecommunications manufacturer Huawei, as well as two affiliated companies and Huawei’s Chief Financial Officer Wanzhou Meng, during a press conference at the Department of Justice in Washington, DC, January 28, 2019",
              "The Canada-China relationship has steadily degraded since the arrest. In apparent retaliation, Beijing security forces detained two Canadians in December, causing some of Canada’s allies, including the U.S., United Kingdom, France and Germany, to make public statements in solidarity with the Canadian government. A third Canadian convicted of drug smuggling under Chinese law was abruptly sentenced to death earlier this month",
              "Experts have pointed out that McCallum is likely right about some of the arguments Meng could use — that the extradition process, for example, was tainted by comments from U.S. President Donald Trump, who suggested in December that Meng could be a pawn in a broader trade war with China",
              "Secretary of Homeland Security Kirstjen Nielson speaks alongside Acting US Attorney General Matthew Whitaker (L) and FBI Director Christopher Wray as they announce a 13-count indictment of financial fraud charges against Chinese telecommunications manufacturer Huawei, as well as two affiliated companies and Huawei’s Chief Financial Officer Wanzhou Meng, during a press conference at the Department of Justice in Washington,"
            ]
          },
          {
            "url": "https://www.kpbs.org/news/2019/jan/28/us-charges-chinese-telecom-giant-huawei-asks/",
            "title": "U.S. Charges Chinese Telecom Giant Huawei, Asks Canada For CFO ...",
            "snippets": [
              "# U.S. Charges Chinese Telecom Giant Huawei, Asks Canada For CFO Extradition\nA woman browses her smartphone as she walks by a Huawei store at a shopping mall in Beijing. The telecom giant is at the center of a geopolitical feud between China and the United States.\n ET**\n\nThe Justice Department unsealed criminal charges against China's most important telecommunications company on Monday, in a deepening of the ongoing geopolitical chill across the Pacific Ocean",
              "Acting Attorney General Matthew Whitaker said Huawei has been indicted on 13 criminal counts and that he is requesting that Canada extradite its chief financial officer, Meng Wanzhou, who was arrested in Vancouver on Dec. 1 on a U.S. warrant.",
              "Meng was released on bail but has not been able to leave Canada given the expectation that Washington might ask to put her on trial. The Wall Street Journal reported last April that the Justice Department was investigating Huawei for potentially violating U.S. sanctions on Iran.\n\nWhitaker said that Huawei has been trying to steal American telecommunications technology and also lying about its relationship with its Iranian subsidiary. The Chinese parent company claimed that it had sold its Iranian division, he said, when actually it had kept control and ownership of it",
              "The announcement on Monday was the latest example of the Justice Department raising the alarm over what it calls the loss of billions of dollars' worth of American intellectual property to Chinese cybertheft.\nBecome a KPBS sponsor\n\nU.S. and allied intelligence officials also fear that Huawei's technology helps with Chinese government espionage. It has been barred from selling network equipment in countries leery about Beijing's ambitions",
              "Trudeau fired Ottawa's ambassador to China after the envoy mused about the case that Meng might make to keep from being extradited to the United States and about how much better for Canada it might be if the United States dropped its extradition request.\n\nCanada's foreign minister said the former ambassador's comments had made it untenable for him to remain in his job, according to Reuters.\n\nWith sanctions and the threat of more economic restrictions, President Trump has sought to pressure China into accepting broader concessions in the bilateral trading relationship",
              "A delegation from China is expected in Washington as part of the ongoing trade dispute, but White House press secretary Sarah Sanders said on Monday that the charges announced at the Justice Department are not a negotiation tactic.\n\n\"It's a totally separate process,\" she said.\n\nWhitaker also was asked about the broader bilateral situation between the United States and China. He said the Department of Justice acts independently of the White House and it had arrived at the need to charge Huawei as the result of its own investigations",
              "Canadian officials also have sought to separate themselves from the U.S.-China tensions, arguing there's no cause for China to detain the Canadians it has taken into custody since Meng's arrest.\n\nChina's ambassador to Canada shot back earlier this month that Ottawa is guilty of \"white supremacy\" and of applying a double standard in asking for its citizens to be free while Meng remained unable to travel and subject to extradition to the United States."
            ]
          },
          {
            "url": "https://www.npr.org/2019/01/28/689457275/u-s-charges-chinese-telecom-giant-huawei-asks-canada-for-cfo-extradition",
            "title": "U.S. Charges Chinese Telecom Giant Huawei, Asks Canada For CFO ...",
            "snippets": [
              "- Keyboard shortcuts for audio player\n\n**U.S. Charges Chinese Telecom Giant Huawei, Asks Canada For CFO Extradition** **The administration appears to have decided to make an example of the company's CFO. She is the daughter of Huawei's founder, and the company is seen as an arm of China's power around the world.**",
              "# U.S. Charges Chinese Telecom Giant Huawei, Asks Canada For CFO Extradition\nA woman browses her smartphone as she walks by a Huawei store at a shopping mall in Beijing. The telecom giant is at the center of a geopolitical feud between China and the United States.\n ET**\n\nThe Justice Department unsealed criminal charges against China's most important telecommunications company on Monday, in a deepening of the ongoing geopolitical chill across the Pacific Ocean",
              "### A 3rd Canadian Has Been Detained In China As Diplomatic Rancor Rises\nBeijing is expected to condemn the announcement. The Chinese government already has responded to Meng's arrest by taking a number of Canadians into custody and applying pressure on its relationship with Ottawa.\n\nSo far, the government of Prime Minister Justin Trudeau has held fast, although cracks have begun to appear.",
              "### Huawei Founder Denies His Firm Spies For China\nWith sanctions and the threat of more economic restrictions, President Trump has sought to pressure China into accepting broader concessions in the bilateral trading relationship.\n\nA delegation from China is expected in Washington as part of the ongoing trade dispute, but White House press secretary Sarah Sanders said on Monday that the charges announced at the Justice Department are not a negotiation tactic.\n\n\"It's a totally separate process,\" she said",
              "U.S. Will Seek To Extradite Huawei CFO Meng Wanzhou, Canadian Diplomat Says"
            ]
          },
          {
            "url": "https://www.cnbc.com/2019/01/28/us-seeks-extradition-of-huawei-cfo-meng-wanzhou.html",
            "title": "US files criminal charges in two Huawei cases, seeks extradition ...",
            "snippets": [
              "# US files criminal charges in two Huawei cases, seeks extradition of CFO Meng Wanzhou\n- The U.S. Justice Department plans to formally request the extradition of Huawei's CFO from Canada.\n- The arrest of Meng Wanzhou Dec. 1 in Vancouver has caused a two-month-long series of tense exchanges between China and Canada over the possibility that she could be transferred to the U.S.\n- China has strongly opposed both the arrest and the prospect of extradition.\n- The Justice Department also alleged Huawei stole trade secrets related to a phone-testing robot from T-Mobile",
              "DOJ charges Huawei with fraud, seeks extradition of CFO\n\nwatch now\n\nVIDEO 3:15 03:15\n\nDOJ charges Huawei with fraud, seeks extradition of CFO\n\nClosing Bell\n\nThe U.S. Department of Justice filed criminal charges Monday against Meng Wanzhou, the chief financial officer of China tech giant Huawei and the daughter of its founder and president Ren Zhengfei",
              "The Justice Department also announced charges Monday against Huawei for allegedly stealing trade secrets from T-Mobile. The charges stem from a civil trade secrets lawsuit filed by T-Mobile in 2014 over a robot called \"Tappy,\" which was used in testing smartphones.\n\nActing Attorney General Matthew Whitaker said during a press conference that the Justice Department is seeking the extradition of Meng Wanzhou from Canada",
              "\"We plan to file our formal extradition request and all the necessary documentation under the extradition treaty in the appropriate time frame,\" Whitaker said.\n\nThe Justice Department formally filed its request for extradition on Monday, Canadian authorities confirmed to CNBC. There was a Jan. 30 deadline for the request to be submitted.\n\nHuawei said in a statement provided to CNBC that it was \"disappointed to learn of the charges brought against the company today.\" Here's the rest of that statement:",
              "China, meanwhile, said through an industry ministry official that the indictment against the company is unfair and immoral, according to Reuters.\n\nFBI, DHS and Commerce officials announced the two actions on Monday, saying the allegations go back more than a decade. \"Huawei and its senior executives repeatedly refused to respect U.S. law,\" said FBI Director Christopher Wray, in Monday's press conference. \"Huawei ... systematically sought to steal valuable trade secrets.\"",
              "## Alleged theft from T-Mobile\nIn its previous civil case against Huawei for the alleged thefts, Huawei said the robot was \"common knowledge\" and that it didn't steal T-Mobile's trade secrets.\n\n*Correction: The Department of Justice plans to file extradition paperwork in the Meng Wanzhou case by a Jan. 30 deadline.*",

⚠️ [... middle content omitted — showing head and tail ...]

              "A grand jury in Seattle has charged Huawei with conspiracy to steal trade secrets, attempted theft of trade secrets, seven counts of wire fraud and one count of obstruction of justice for the company’s alleged attempts to move potential witnesses back to China. The indictments grew out of a civil suit dating all the way back to 2014 in which T-Mobile sued Huawei for stealing trade secrets related to a robotic phone-testing device known as “Tappy.”"
            ]
          }
        ],
        "map": []
